  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of immune signaling pathways centered on B cells and T cells, highlighting therapeutic targets that interrupt these interactions. A plasmacytoid dendritic cell at the top produces type I interferon, which can be blocked either upstream with anti-BDCA2 or downstream with a JAK inhibitor. The B cell is shown as a major hub: its B-cell receptor signals through BTK, and this pathway is inhibited by a BTK inhibitor. The B cell also expresses CD20, targeted by anti-CD20, and receives survival signals through BAFFR and TACI from the ligands BAFF and APRIL; these are blocked by anti-BAFF, anti-BAFFR, or anti-TACI.\n\nCommunication between B cells and T cells is represented through CD40 on the B cell interacting with CD40L on the T cell, with anti-CD40 blocking this costimulatory signal. On the T-cell side, IL-12 signaling through IL-12R is inhibited by ustekinumab, and JAK inhibition also appears to reduce downstream IFNγ signaling or production.\n\nOverall, the image summarizes a network of inflammatory and lymphocyte-activating pathways and shows where different biologic agents or kinase inhibitors can intervene to dampen immune activation.",
